A||N||7||kornholio||wheel balancing||||1||
Z||000000||kornholio||02-16-2001||12:59 PM||||What are the symptoms of an unbalanced wheel?  I suspect that one of the little weights came off of one of the wheels.  Is there a noticeable noise when this happens?||||reg||1
Z||000001||SteveJ||02-16-2001||01:15 PM||||The wheel begins to vibrate at about 40 MPH and has increasing violence as it goes faster. Sometimes you get them where they vibrate badly at 40 but seem to smooth out at 55 to 60. Dynamics or harmonics or the increased frequency of the vibration give the impression that it's smoothing up but its just less noticable and doing a lot of harm to the structure. Contrary to some of the posts I have seem here loose bushings or joints don't cause this type of vibration they just wear the hell out of the tires.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SteveJ||02-16-2001||01:16 PM||||Oh yea, noise? Nope. Why what kind of noise you getting?||||reg||1
Z||000003||SteveJ||02-16-2001||01:29 PM||||Have some fun while you are driving down the freeway sometimes by watching other people's wheels. Sometimes you see 'em vibrate like crazy but it doesn't seem to bother the drivers. Then when comming up from behind you can sight along from their back tire to the front and see 'em badly out of alignment. . .  and from his blind spot its fun to spot the ones with camber problems as the wheel is at such a crazy angle leaning way in or way out. And again they are oblivious and relate their poor tire mileage to quality problems.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||02-16-2001||05:40 PM||||Yes the vibration is like he said usually between 40's-60s. Generally if the steering wheels is shaking its in the front, if you feel the shaking in your azz prob the rear ones. The very best way to balance is on the car with a jack that has a powered drum-but its almost impossible to find anyone with one anymore. They actualy spin the whole tire, rim, rotor to balance the whole thing at hiway speed. I never had that good a luck with computerized balance. Ive always ended up getting them redone. Also, an out of round wheel and/or tire can give you the same symtoms.||||reg||1
Z||000005||kornholio||02-16-2001||08:04 PM||||Well, it looks like its not wheel balancing that my car needs.  It's the control arm bushings that were worn.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||02-17-2001||01:03 AM||||A quick check to see if a tire is badly out of balance (like from loosing a weight)... Jack it up, spin the wheel several times and mark where it stops each time. If it's balanced the marks will be pretty much random locations. If it threw a weight off, it will stop the same place most of the time.||||reg||3
Z||000007||SteveJ||02-17-2001||09:22 AM||||OK, the trick my grandfather taught me about the way they used to balance model t's. Find a wheel that rotates freely, usually the front. Take off the caliper. Loosen the wheel bearing if needed to make it really swing free. Mount the wheel. Then give the wheel a slight swing. If its in balance like DRH says it will stop at random points. But usually not so when it stops at the same point repeatedly take two weights and put them at the top so they just stick on. Repeatedly try until you get it right. Then put one weight on the inside and one on the outside of the wheel to keep its dynamic balance due to the weights even.<BR>This is a good way to get a "just about" balance and will hold until you can get to a computer balance place.<BR>In the old days before 4-wheel independent suspension this type of balance was good enough for the rear axle that was one huge piece of unsprung metal.<BR>Couple of years ago I bought a Corsica, which came complete with vibration so I swung each wheel and it was only one of them that was out of balance. So I did my thing and put that tire on the back and it hasn’t bothered me since.<BR>Wheel weights are cheap . . .on the ground in any parking lot or if you live in a city you spot 'em on the ground at the corner of blocks. The stress of turning must pop the marginal ones off.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 02-17-2001).]||||reg||1
